            <div xml:id="d450-a1">

                <pb facs="0824" n="762" sameAs="frus:frus1878/pg762"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 42.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. de Giers to Mr. Hoffman.</hi></head>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: By his note of the 18–28th March last Mr.
                    Stoughton renewed his reclamation regarding the differences which exist in the
                    Russian tariff as regards the import duties on merchandise according to its
                    importation by land or by sea, insisting on the injury which resulted therefrom
                    to the commerce of the subjects of the United States, and on the violation which
                    might he found therein of the spirit and letter of the treaty of 1832.</p>
                <p>The imperial ministry has devoted to the contents of this new note an attentive
                    consideration, hut to their great regret do not find good reasons for sharing,
                    in this affair, the views of the Government of the United States of America.</p>
                <p>Accept, sir, &amp;c.</p>
                <closer><signed>GIERS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
